# More than one JS lockfile in one directory means two package managers claim
# the same project: CI picks one (or guesses), the developer uses the other, and
# the installed versions differ. A CI install step that detects this fails
# late; this rule says it at commit time instead.
#
# npm-shrinkwrap.json is deliberately NOT a target: npm allows it next to
# package-lock.json (the shrinkwrap wins), so that pair counts as one npm lockfile.
# Bun counts once: only bun.lock (the text format since Bun 1.2) is a target. The
# legacy binary bun.lockb next to it is the same package manager, and max-dir-files
# counts different file names as different units, so it is not listed. Limit: a
# repo with only bun.lockb next to another PM's lockfile is not detected.
#
# Engine max-dir-files groups the matched files PER DIRECTORY and runs on the full
# repo fileset in the hook (ADR-052), so a monorepo with one package manager per
# package is fine, and the pair is found even when only one lockfile is staged.
#
# @see docs/rules-authoring.md (max-dir-files), ADR-052 (file-level scope).
id: NODE-LOCK-001
ilities: [reliability-maturity, integrity]
maturity: 2
title: More than one JS lockfile in one directory
category: supply-chain
severity: error
engine: max-dir-files
action: block
min_count: 1
target:
  filenames: package-lock.json pnpm-lock.yaml yarn.lock bun.lock
  exclude_paths: node_modules/ .git/ vendor/
patterns:
  - regex: more than one lockfile
    message: "more than one JS lockfile in this directory (package-lock.json, pnpm-lock.yaml, yarn.lock, bun.lock): keep the one of the package manager you use and delete the others"
fix: "Delete the lockfiles of the package managers you do not use, and set \"packageManager\" in package.json so tools and CI agree."
